preConditionHook:
// A BAR-BY-BAR TRAIL THAT DOES NOT ARM UNTIL THE TRADE HAS SHOWN A MINIMUM
// open profit. Before that the wider initial stop (a separate placement) stands
// alone; after it, a close beyond the PRIOR bar's extreme takes the trade off.
//
// R29-14 states this as a GATE on other tightening rules -- "do not tighten
// until about four ticks of profit" -- which the pane system cannot express,
// since exits are ORed and one exit cannot condition another. The only
// mechanizable shape is the single armed-trail exit written here, the same
// recast NumBarsIfProfitable makes for the profit-gated time stop.
//
// ARMING IS ctx.MaxPositionProfit(), NOT A HAND-ROLLED FLAG: it is VERIFIED as
// the INTRABAR peak, net of the entry side's costs, starting at 0 per position
// and restarting through a reversal, so it is already the once-true-per-position
// latch this rule needs and nothing has to be reset. armProfitDollars is
// CURRENCY, not ticks -- the source's four ticks is a per-symbol statement of
// the same idea, and the dollar form is the corpus's threshold currency.
//
// The tick offset IS ticks: MinMove/PriceScale are VERIFIED and the tick is
// written division-first, stopOffsetTicks * (ctx.MinMove() / ctx.PriceScale()),
// computed once per bar into a local since both are per-symbol constants.
//
// SIDES ARE MIRRORED, correcting the merged `same`: the long reads the prior
// bar's low and the short the prior bar's high, which is directional. The same
// correction BreakevenStop and BreakevenExitAfterAdverseExcursion needed.
//
// Every scratch value is a localVariables entry, never a hook-scope
// declaration, so the hooks survive a double placement.
//
// Deepest bar touched: 1.
tickSize = ctx.MinMove() / ctx.PriceScale();
armed = el_ge(ctx.MaxPositionProfit(), armProfitDollars);
